# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows checkout rewrites line
# endings on the way out of the repo. What gets rewritten is decided by a heuristic that
# sniffs the first few KB for NUL bytes. That heuristic would very probably get all of
# this right -- but "very probably", across 63 data files, on a laptop we have never
# seen, an hour before a demo, is not a thing to leave to chance. So it is stated.

# Market data. Never touch these bytes: a single LF->CRLF substitution inside a parquet
# file corrupts it, and the failure surfaces as an unreadable dataset, not as a warning.
*.parquet binary
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.pdf      binary

# The tick logs are the record the entire benchmark is recomputed from, and the replay
# verifier rebuilds every number from them. Pinned to LF so a Windows checkout is
# byte-identical to a Linux one.
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# Source and docs: LF everywhere, so a Windows contributor cannot accidentally commit a
# whole-file diff that is nothing but invisible carriage returns.
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf
*.cfg  text eol=lf
